# Binaries for programs and plugins
*.exe
*.exe~
*.dll
*.so
*.dylib
/objectfs
objectfs-*

# C SDK build artifacts
sdks/c/tests/test_basic
sdks/c/tests/test_mount

# `go build ./sdks/c/` — the obvious way to check that package compiles — writes a binary named
# after its directory, so a 44 MB file called `c` lands at the repo root. Ignored rather than
# discouraged: the command is the right one to run, and the artifact is not obviously an artifact.
/c

# Python SDK
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
*.egg-info/

# JavaScript SDK
node_modules/

# Test binary, built with `go test -c`
*.test

# Output of the go coverage tool, specifically when used with LiteIDE
*.out

# Dependency directories (remove the comment below to include it)
# vendor/

# Go workspace file
go.work

# IDE files
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo
*~

# OS generated files
.DS_Store
.DS_Store?
._*
.Spotlight-V100
.Trashes
ehthumbs.db
Thumbs.db

# Log files
*.log
logs/

# Cache directories
.cache/
/cache/

# Build and distribution directories
dist/
build/
bin/

# TypeDoc's output, for sdks/javascript. `npm run docs` writes ./docs, and like `target/` below,
# nothing had ever run it — so nothing had produced the directory to notice it was untracked. It is
# anchored, unlike the entries above, because `docs/` unanchored would swallow the repository's own
# `docs/` tree.
#
# Leaving it untracked-but-not-ignored is worse than either alternative, and not hypothetically:
# TypeDoc copies CONTRIBUTING.md into docs/media/, and TestDocumentedLinksResolve then reads that
# copy's relative links against the wrong base directory and fails on a link that is correct in the
# original.
/sdks/javascript/docs/

# Maven's output directory, for sdks/java. Nothing had ever run `mvn` in this repository — that is
# why the Java SDK shipped four compile errors — so nothing had ever produced this directory to
# notice it was untracked. `target/` and not `sdks/java/target/`, matching the unanchored style of
# the three above.
target/

# Coverage reports
coverage.txt
coverage.html
coverage.out
# The directory the Makefile's `coverage` target writes into (COVERAGE_DIR := coverage). The three
# bare filenames above only match a profile written to the repo root, which `make coverage` does not
# do — it writes coverage/coverage.out and coverage/coverage.html.
coverage/

# Temporary files
tmp/
temp/

# Configuration files with secrets
config/local.yaml
config/secrets.yaml
*.env
.env*

# Mount points (for testing)
/mnt/
/tmp/objectfs-*

# Benchmark results
*.bench
benchmark-results/

# What benchmarks/run_benchmarks.sh actually writes. The line above names benchmark-results/, which
# nothing creates — so every run left an untracked directory of timestamped result files, a baseline
# and a summary, ready for the next `git add -A` to commit them.
benchmarks/results/

# Profile data
*.prof
*.pprof

# Docker
.dockerignore

# Kubernetes
*.kubeconfig